<h2 id="architecture">Architecture at a glance</h2>
<p>Booster Auth is made of a few services that all run with a single <code>docker compose</code>. Only two doors are open to the outside: the HTTPS
    gateway and the admin console. Infrastructure services stay on the internal network.</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Gateway</b></td><td>The single HTTPS entry point; routes each request to the right service.</td><td><code>https://auth.company.com</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>IdP</b></td><td>OpenID Connect identity provider: sign-in, sign-up, tokens.</td><td><code>/idp</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Management API</b></td><td>Tenants, pools, applications, roles; licensing.</td><td><code>/management</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>AuthZ</b></td><td>Authorization schema and permission checks (on OpenFGA).</td><td><code>/authz</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Admin console</b></td><td>The web interface for administrators (English / Turkish).</td><td><code>http://localhost:3000</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka, OpenFGA; never exposed.</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout tip"><p><b>Quick start.</b> Just want to try it? <a href="/en/download">Download the package</a> and follow the steps on the
    <a href="/en/docs/installation">Installation</a> page: the script generates the secrets and <code>docker compose up -d</code> starts the system.</p></div>
